   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The functions \\( f \\) and \\( g \\) are differentiable, with \\( f(2) = 5 \\) and \\( f'(2) = -1 \\). The line tangent to the graph of \\( g \\) at \\( x = 2 \\) has equation \\( y = x \\). Let \\( h(x) = \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} \\). Find the value of \\( h'(2) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         -7,
         4
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Record the values at \\( x = 2 \\): \\( f(2) = 5 \\), \\( f'(2) = -1 \\). The tangent line gives \\( g(2) = 2 \\) (its height at \\( x = 2 \\)) and \\( g'(2) = 1 \\) (its slope).",
         "mathjson": -1
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "By the quotient rule, \\( \\left(\\frac{f}{g}\\right)'(2) = \\frac{f'(2)g(2) - f(2)g'(2)}{[g(2)]^{2}} \\).",
         "mathjson": 5
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Substitute: \\( \\frac{(-1)(2) - (5)(1)}{(2)^{2}} = \\frac{-7}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            -7,
            4
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( h'(2) = - \\frac{7}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            -7,
            4
         ]
      }
   ],
